# Target C-II — host margin makes exact-force bilateral birth gap-free

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** exact-antipodal force pairs on one strict side of the common hub, through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** exact-antipodal exact force-pair theorem; coupled host-margin sharpening; one-sided path-region switching; full-source diameter-midpoint localization; Cauchy perimeter-diameter inequality  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_gap_free_bilateral_force_weave_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The existing bilateral-birth theorem required a fixed positive projective gap between the two force bars. That hypothesis is unnecessary once the host-derived transverse margin is combined with the **ordinary oriented** force-pair geometry.

At an exact-antipodal interface, write the reaction axis as

\[
e_i={G_i\over |G_i|}.
\]

The exact positive force-pair theorem chooses two switching diameter directions `d_i^-`,`d_i^+` in one common open semicircle, with ordinary lifted angles

\[
\alpha_i-p_i,\qquad \alpha_i+q_i,
\qquad p_i,q_i>0,\qquad p_i+q_i<\pi,
\tag{GB.1}
\]

about the ordinary angle `alpha_i` of `e_i`. The sharpened host theorem gives every switching diameter the projective row-axis margin

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([d],[e_i])\ge \gamma_H-o(1),
\qquad
\gamma_H:=\arcsin c_H,
\tag{GB.2}
\]

where

\[
c_H=0.453819885927\ldots .
\tag{GB.3}
\]

Since `p_i,q_i in (0,pi)`, their projective distances from the axis are

\[
\min\{p_i,\pi-p_i\},\qquad \min\{q_i,\pi-q_i\}.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{p_i,q_i\ge\gamma_H-o(1)}
\tag{GB.4}
\]

and the **ordinary** angle between the two consistently oriented force rays obeys

\[
\boxed{
2\gamma_H-o(1)
\le \Delta_i:=p_i+q_i<\pi.
}
\tag{GB.5}
\]

Numerically `2 gamma_H=53.97805857... degrees`. Thus a force pair may become projectively narrow only by approaching the antipodal end `Delta_i -> pi`; it can never become an ordinary narrow pair.

This distinction yields a gap-free bilateral theorem and a two-lane crossing theorem.

---

## 1. Every exact force pair has macroscopic birth and partner rungs

Orient each selected switching diameter from its hubward endpoint toward its birth endpoint. Write

\[
d_i^\sigma={x_i^\sigma-y_i^\sigma\over D},
\qquad \sigma\in\{-,+\},
\tag{GB.6}
\]

where `x_i^sigma` lies in the birth layer and `y_i^sigma` lies hubward. Let

\[
m_i^\sigma={x_i^\sigma+y_i^\sigma\over2}
\]

be its midpoint. The full-source midpoint theorem gives one common circumcenter `o` and

\[
|m_i^\sigma-o|<L_D,
\qquad L_D=O(D^{3/4})=o(D).
\tag{GB.7}
\]

Using

\[
x_i^\sigma=m_i^\sigma+{D\over2}d_i^\sigma,
\qquad
y_i^\sigma=m_i^\sigma-{D\over2}d_i^\sigma,
\]

we obtain

\[
\begin{aligned}
|x_i^- -x_i^+|
&\ge {D\over2}|d_i^- -d_i^+|-|m_i^- -m_i^+|\\
&>D\sin{\Delta_i\over2}-2L_D,
\end{aligned}
\tag{GB.8}
\]

and identically

\[
|y_i^- -y_i^+|>D\sin{\Delta_i\over2}-2L_D.
\tag{GB.9}
\]

Because `Delta_i/2 in [gamma_H-o(1),pi/2)`, sine is increasing there. Hence (GB.2)--(GB.5) give

\[
\boxed{
|x_i^- -x_i^+|,
|y_i^- -y_i^+|
\ge(c_H-o(1))D-2L_D
=(c_H-o(1))D.
}
\tag{GB.10}
\]

This lower bound is independent of the projective pair gap. In particular every exact force pair has two macroscopically separated birth endpoints and two macroscopically separated hubward endpoints.

The four endpoints are distinct for all sufficiently large `D`: the two births and two partners are separated by (GB.10), while a birth endpoint and a hubward endpoint lie in different path regions on a strict one-sided interface. Consequently the two actual diameter bars are vertex-disjoint and, by the convex diameter-thrackle property, cross geometrically.

---

## 2. Only six exact-force interfaces can be born twice on one host side

For an internal birth layer `R_i`, let `C_i^+`,`C_i^-` be the two outer-boundary host subarcs between the consecutive O--O crosscuts. Every switching diameter has exactly one birth endpoint in the layer, hence every force-pair birth belongs to

\[
x_i^\pm\in C_i^+\cup C_i^-.
\tag{GB.11}
\]

Suppose both force bars are born on the same host subarc, say `C_i^+`. The subarc between the two birth vertices has length at least their Euclidean separation. By (GB.10),

\[
\operatorname{len}(C_i^+)\ge(c_H-o(1))D.
\tag{GB.12}
\]

The layer host subarcs have pairwise disjoint interiors as `i` varies. The full convex source hull has perimeter at most `pi D`. Hence if `N_uni(D)` exact-force interfaces have both births on the same host side,

\[
N_{\rm uni}(D)(c_H-o(1))D\le\pi D.
\tag{GB.13}
\]

Since

\[
7c_H-\pi=0.03514654789\ldots>0,
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
\boxed{N_{\rm uni}(D)\le6.}
\tag{GB.14}
\]

Thus **every exact-antipodal force family is bilateral after deleting at most six interfaces**, with no fixed pair-gap hypothesis and no density loss.

This strictly strengthens the previous `O_rho(1)` bilateral theorem on the exact-force population. The old fixed-gap theorem remains useful when a macroscopic central diamond radius depending on `rho` is required.

---

## 3. Coherently oriented force lanes have no cross-sign endpoint collisions

Fix any exact-force subfamily on one strict side of the common hub for which the **ordinary** reaction axes lie in one shrinking angular window. This hypothesis holds automatically on every exact-phase block. It also holds on at least half of any projectively phase-collapsed exact-force population: choose a reference representative of the common projective phase; the ordinary reaction axes have only the two antipodal lifts, so one lift contains at least half the interfaces.

Orient every reaction axis in that common ordinary lift and label each force pair so that

\[
d_i^-\cdot Je_i<0<d_i^+\cdot Je_i.
\tag{GB.15}
\]

By (GB.4), any `-` force direction and any `+` force direction in this coherently oriented subfamily have ordinary angular separation at least

\[
2\gamma_H-o(1).
\tag{GB.16}
\]

Indeed, write the ordinary reaction angles as `alpha_i=alpha_*+o(1)`. The force-pair lift gives

\[
\arg d_i^- = \alpha_i-p_i,
\qquad
\arg d_i^+ = \alpha_i+q_i,
\]

with `p_i,q_i>=gamma_H-o(1)`. Hence

\[
\arg d_j^+-\arg d_i^-
\ge2\gamma_H-o(1)
\]

in the common semicircle lift.

Now suppose `h_i^-` and `h_j^+` shared an endpoint. For `i=j`, this contradicts the four-endpoint conclusion of Section 1. For `i\ne j`, births belong to distinct pre-hub path regions, so the shared endpoint can only be the common hubward partner `y`.

Let the other endpoints be `x_i,x_j`. Since both `yx_i` and `yx_j` are actual diameter edges,

\[
{x_i+y\over2},\ {x_j+y\over2}\in B(o,L_D).
\]

Therefore

\[
|x_i-x_j|<4L_D.
\tag{GB.17}
\]

The angle `vartheta` between the two rays from `y` satisfies

\[
2D\sin{\vartheta\over2}=|x_i-x_j|<4L_D,
\]

so

\[
\vartheta<2\arcsin{2L_D\over D}=o(1).
\tag{GB.18}
\]

This contradicts (GB.16). Thus, for all sufficiently large `D`,

\[
\boxed{
V(h_i^-)\cap V(h_j^+)=\varnothing
\quad\text{throughout every coherently oriented exact-force subfamily.}
}
\tag{GB.19}
\]

Every minus-lane bar is therefore vertex-disjoint from every plus-lane bar in that subfamily. The convex diameter-thrackle property gives

\[
\boxed{
\text{every minus-lane force bar crosses every plus-lane force bar.}
}
\tag{GB.20}
\]

Same-sign partner stars are not excluded. Nor is a global ordinary-axis lift asserted when the common projective phase approaches the boundary of the one-sided reaction semicircle. The unconditional statement is instead: every exact-phase block has the complete bipartite crossing weave, and any projectively collapsed population contains a subfamily of at least half its size with that property.

---

## 4. Consequence for growing exact-force diagonals

Apply Sections 1--3 to the near-full-shore growing exact-force diagonal. Its projective pair gap `rho_D` is allowed to tend to zero. That no longer weakens bilateral birth or the two-lane crossing geometry.

After deleting at most six center interfaces over the entire shore, every retained exact-force center has

\[
\boxed{
|x_i^- -x_i^+|,
|y_i^- -y_i^+|
\ge(c_H-o(1))D,
}
\tag{GB.21}
\]

one birth on each physical host arc, and every growing exact-phase neighborhood has the complete crossing relation (GB.20). Globally, at least half of the centers may be chosen with one coherent ordinary reaction-axis lift and hence one complete cross-sign weave. These conclusions coexist with the growing exact-phase depth, vanishing normalized switching/KKT complexity, exact positive two-ray force identities, common train grid, and fixed individual transverse derivative margin already proved on that diagonal.

The shrinking projective pair gap has a precise remaining meaning: it controls whether the two ordinary force rays approach the antipodal end `Delta_i=pi`, and therefore controls the radius of the central four-endpoint diamond. It does **not** allow the two birth endpoints, the two partner endpoints, or the two ordinary oriented force rays to collapse together.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The no-TB1 exact-force obstruction can now be viewed as two macroscopic endpoint lanes rather than a collection of possibly degenerating local pairs:

```text
one force bar born on each physical host at all but <=6 interfaces;
birth rungs and partner rungs have length >=(0.4538198859-o(1))D;
minus and plus lanes are completely crossing on every exact-phase block;
projective pair gap may still tend to zero only through near-antipodality;
all existing exact-force/KKT/common-grid metadata is retained.
```

This removes the fixed-angle hypothesis from bilateral loading and removes cross-sign endpoint stars as an escape on every coherent exact-phase block. It does not close C-II: a near-antipodal pair can still have projectively close unoriented lines while its endpoints remain macroscopically separated. The remaining geometric task is to reconcile this two-lane macroscopic weave with the paired nested destination order and the common near-equilateral train grid, or to turn that incompatibility into a source-preserving strict diameter decrease.

**Disposition:** proved gap-free bilateral birth, macroscopic birth/partner rung separation, and a complete cross-sign crossing weave on coherent exact-force blocks. C-II and Level I remain open.
